# The Poet-Queen's Passing!

The late Sarojini was an exemplar of the distinction, fame, and intellectual ability of the women of our country. She played a major part not only in the national liberation struggle but also in working to remove social degradation. Believing that the bonds of slavery imposed in the name of caste had first to be broken before a country enslaved by others could be liberated, Sarojini herself entered into an inter-caste marriage.

In 1898, she married Comrade M. G. Naidu in Madras, who is now a major in Hyderabad. Unlike today, that was a time when an inter-caste marriage was regarded as deadly poison. Opposition arose from both the bride's and the groom's families. Nevertheless, the marriage took place happily.

She began participating in political work only in 1915. From then until the end, she devoted her entire attention to the country's liberation. Though imprisoned several times and subjected to many hardships, she continued her work with an undaunted heart.

During the years when she fought for national freedom, she served as President of the Congress, a member of its Working Committee, India's representative to Africa, the representative of Indian women at the Round Table Conference, and President of the Asian Relations Conference.

In recognition of her incomparable national service, the Government of India appointed her Governor of the United Provinces. While serving in that office and performing her duty, she died suddenly of a heart attack on 2 March 1949. Her death is an irreparable loss to the country.

The late Sarojini was accomplished not only in social reform and national liberation. Her learning made her known throughout the world. Her education had developed to such a degree that she could compose poetry at the age of eleven. She possessed unequalled skill in composing poems rich in ideas, and thus received the title "Poet-Queen." *The Golden Threshold*, *The Bird of Time*, and *The Broken Wing* are among the finest of her works.

The name of this lady, who served the country without abandoning to the end the noble qualities of knowledge, fearlessness, and courage, deserves a place in history.

We convey our sympathy and consolation to her grieving husband, Major M. Govindarajulu, to her children, and to the people of the country.

(*Dravida Nadu*, 6-3-49)
